# Line endings are pinned here rather than left to each machine's Git settings.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its *system* config, so without this
# file a Windows checkout gets CRLF while Linux and macOS CI get LF. That works right
# up until a contributor has `core.autocrlf=false`, at which point a file committed on
# Windows keeps its CRLF and every later diff against it is the whole file.
#
# `eol=lf` takes precedence over `core.autocrlf`, so this makes a Windows checkout and
# a Linux one produce identical bytes. The repository is explicitly cross-platform:
# developed on Windows, built and tested on all three in CI.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one place CRLF is genuinely wanted: scripts Windows will run itself.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binaries: never diffed, never converted. Listing them keeps `git diff` from
# claiming "Binary files differ" in a way that hides a real change, and stops the
# text filters from being applied to an archive.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff2 binary
*.rgba binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.crate binary

# Lockfiles are generated; collapsing them in review is noise, and GitHub renders
# them poorly. They are still text, so they diff normally — this only helps tooling.
Cargo.lock text eol=lf
